  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Hydnum albidum, commonly known as the white hedgehog, is a small edible fungus characterized by its pale white to cream-colored fruiting body and distinctive tooth-like spines beneath the cap. This species belongs to the Hydnaceae family, a group of fungi identified by their spine-bearing undersides rather than traditional gills.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "White coloration",
      "description": "Fruiting body is uniformly white to pale cream throughout, including the cap, stem, and spines",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Spines instead of gills",
      "description": "Distinctive tooth-like structures (spines or hydnoid spines) hang down from the underside of the cap rather than radiating gills",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "Typically 1-3 inches in height with a delicate, fragile appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Hydnum albidum grows on the forest floor in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, particularly in moist areas with rich soil and abundant leaf litter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its delicate appearance, the white hedgehog is edible and considered a choice species in some regions, though it requires careful cleaning due to its fragile spines that easily trap soil and debris.",
